title: many ideas
pid: 3eda0169-b0ef-4c56-8f9d-c398b3729574
body: |2-
  I will probably read another's data depth-first,
  and would like to be able to store which branches I have processed.
  Forking and subscription basically affect one layer of contentward
  relationships, which feels awkward.
  If I subscribe my A' to your A, and your A contains B which contains C,
  and then you add C2 to the content of B,
  I want my subscription to A to inform me of the new C1 and B-contains-C1.
  So the subscription herald should traverse recursively contentward
  to calculate how much new content to report.

  For us to discuss something, we must process our own forks of it
  (one of them likely being the original).
  It is unnatural for me to separately mark every "edit" I make to your note
  (change I make to my fork of it) as needing your attention
  by putting it in a mailbox.
  I do want the mailboxes, but also I'd like to be able to say
  "I've responded granularly to the contentward tree from <origin node>,"
  and you can easily see what my responses are.

  This is the diffview problem, generalized.
  Some plans for it, some of them executed, are at TODO/general-fast-diffView/,
  which we should definitely ingest into these skg notes.

  The natural workflow, it would seem, is:
